---
import ExposedStorageNine from "../../../components/studies/ExposedStorageNine.astro";
import StorageConstruction from "../../../components/studies/StorageConstruction.astro";
import TechnicalStorageNine from "../../../components/studies/TechnicalStorageNine.astro";
import UtilityStorageComparison from "../../../components/studies/UtilityStorageComparison.astro";
import { studies } from "../../../data/studies";
import StudyLayout from "../../../layouts/StudyLayout.astro";

const study = studies.find((entry) => entry.slug === "utilities-2026-11-02");
---

<StudyLayout study={study}>
	<aside aria-label="Study closure">
		<h2>Closed / Failed study</h2>
		<p>
			Closed on 2 November 2026 by the designer: “Let's file this study as
			completely failed and closed loop.” Status is stopped during exploration.
			No utility design was accepted for implementation. Retained examples and
			preferences are evidence of the exploration, not a successful design
			conclusion.
		</p>
		<p>
			The closure evidence is recorded in
			<code>docs/loop-evidence/2026-11-02-utilities-failed-study.md</code>. No
			causal lesson or standing process change is adopted by this closure.
		</p>
	</aside>
	<section>
		<details>
			<summary>01 / Equipment that makes survival legible</summary>
			<p>
				A ship returning from a dangerous pass may have energy left but a
				damaged hull—or an intact hull and almost no power. Utilities should
				make those different needs understandable through both their effects and
				their physical presence. Every chassis has internal energy storage that
				is never visible. The visible storage studied here is optional extra
				capacity. Every ship has, depending on ship type, a solar charger or a
				gravity charger. These are givens for this study; gravity charging is a
				new device to investigate.
			</p>
			<p>
				The four roles are energy storage, solar charging, gravity charging and
				automatic repair. Storage carries a reserve; chargers replenish it;
				repair restores damage. The investigation must establish what each adds
				to flying: when charging is earned, how the two sources differ, and
				whether repair competes for energy. Current ships include a battery and
				solar coil outside their optional slots; that implementation is a
				starting point, not a decision about how the new equipment must be
				mounted.
			</p>
			<p>
				This cycle proposes to settle a coherent role and low-poly identity for
				each utility, including how it reads in different slots above and below
				the hull. Like engines, these should be convincing objects with
				distinctive silhouettes and a few flat planes. Equipment may be compact
				or bulky enough to obscure parts of the chassis and change its
				character: a huge energy store could become the feature the ship is
				known for. Neither restraint nor bulk is settled. Under-hull mounting
				also raises the question of what remains visible from above. Builder
				views and flight scale must both inform the comparison. Exact balance
				values and final chassis assignments remain outside this first decision.
			</p>
			<p>
				Animation is part of the inquiry. A solar charger might unfold wings
				near the sun, a gravity charger might respond visibly inside a gravity
				well, and an empty energy store might blink. These are possibilities,
				not chosen behaviors. Movement could make equipment feel alive while
				revealing its state; the study should explore that appeal alongside
				shape and placement.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>02 / Extra storage as a visible object</h2>
		<details>
			<summary>02.1 / Light and Heavy, above and below</summary>
			<p>
				Reviewed: this polygonal battery treatment does not fit the game’s
				aesthetic. The case and amber-cell design is rejected; the comparison is
				retained as evidence, not a direction to refine. Optional extra storage,
				two possible sizes, and permission to change the ship’s character remain
				part of the inquiry.
			</p>
			<p>
				A chamfered armored case with recessed amber cells proposes a common
				storage identity. Light carries two cells; Heavy broadens into a
				three-cell block. Neither has an engine nozzle. The capacity labels are
				comparison hypotheses relative to the invisible chassis battery; mass is
				only ordered, not tuned.
			</p>
			<p>
				This static fitting sketch uses the production Fighter hull geometry,
				with engines omitted to isolate occlusion. Both modules share the same
				illustrative mount at local (−8, 10). Above and below change drawing
				order only; these are proposed placements, not validated production
				slots. The underside module is honestly hidden where the hull covers it.
				The isolated view explains the concealed object.
			</p>
			<UtilityStorageComparison />
			<p>
				The comparison exposed placement as a design concern alongside the
				device itself. The designer identified Fighter utility slots as needing
				reconsideration after engine-slot changes; moving one forward is a
				possibility, not a settled layout. This fixture omitted engines and used
				an illustrative mount, so it cannot establish production clearances or
				the correct replacement positions. Later fitting comparisons must
				include the actual engine arrangement and slot definitions.
			</p>
		</details>

		<details>
			<summary>02.2 / Shape language before another fitting</summary>
			<p>
				The next inquiry is aesthetic discussion before further variants.
				Engines offer a reference: charcoal bodies with a clear light at the
				outlet. Utilities need an equally deliberate relationship between body,
				silhouette and light, without assuming that a familiar battery icon
				belongs in this world. Whether their forms should suggest grounded
				machinery remains open. They must be identifiable when small and
				compelling when enlarged.
			</p>
			<p>
				The agreed direction is industrial machinery. Utilities may be less
				clean and streamlined than other ship elements: their construction can
				have a physical presence of its own. Industrial does not mean
				deliberately ugly. The designer imagines guns as more pointed objects;
				utilities have room for a different character. That comparison is an
				aesthetic reference, not a settled weapon design.
			</p>
			<p>
				Dark machinery is not an agreed requirement. Color and material
				treatment remain open and will develop with the forms. As a next shape
				hypothesis, a storage device could expose a few substantial cells,
				clamps or a retaining frame, making its construction legible without
				relying on tiny detail. No particular construction is selected yet. New
				bodies, animations and slot changes remain unresolved; this sketch
				establishes neither handling nor charging behavior.
			</p>
		</details>

		<details>
			<summary>02.3 / Two industrial constructions</summary>
			<p>
				These isolated sketches compare an exposed clamped assembly with an open
				retaining frame around the same three cells. Both use identical
				provisional steel tones and small muted status marks; neither palette
				nor cell geometry is accepted. Broad structural parts, gaps and stepped
				ends carry the industrial character rather than surface decoration.
			</p>
			<StorageConstruction />
			<p>
				The small samples test only silhouette and grouping at 32 pixels, not
				actual flight visibility. These are construction alternatives, not Light
				and Heavy sizes. Ship fitting, actual slot clearances, capacity, weight
				and animation are deliberately unresolved in this comparison. The
				rejected fitted example above remains unchanged.
			</p>
		</details>

		<details>
			<summary>02.4 / Exposed machinery, nine small-cell forms</summary>
			<p>
				A from 02.3 is the preferred direction because the machinery itself is
				visible. The next forms have no casing or surrounding protective frame:
				think of exposed equipment attached to a space station. Small mounting
				feet, connectors and working structures may remain; an enclosure should
				not become the object.
			</p>
			<p>
				These nine isolated construction sketches explore a single small
				extra-storage device. A tank, paired vessels, a coil canted at 60°, and
				other exposed assemblies provide different silhouettes. Cell count does
				not set capacity here. All use the same provisional materials, viewing
				scale and static lighting; color, energy technology and physical
				operation remain undecided.
			</p>
			<ExposedStorageNine />
			<p>
				The 32-pixel samples compare silhouette only. These are not fitted ships
				or verified flight-scale renders. Placement with the actual engines,
				Light/Heavy sizing, mass, capacity and animation follow after a useful
				construction direction emerges. Prior examples remain preserved.
			</p>
		</details>

		<details>
			<summary>02.5 / Storage layers with visible machinery</summary>
			<p>
				The pancake stack is close, but neither its enlarged nor small
				appearance is settled. The discarded first 02.5 lost the feeling of
				multiple working parts. This new comparison returns to separated,
				layered containers and investigates their construction: top hardware,
				contacts, recesses, connectors and distinct plate materials.
			</p>
			<p>
				The open helix and unequal pair from 02.4 have useful industrial
				character at small size, but do not clearly suggest an energy reserve to
				the designer. Their construction informs a few variants here while the
				storage layers remain the main body. Deep black, industrial yellow and
				the shared lime-yellow are exploratory accents; no palette or new color
				role is adopted.
			</p>
			<TechnicalStorageNine />
			<p>
				All nine use three layers at the same scale. Shapes and color treatments
				vary together to explore character, not to isolate a single cause. The
				32-pixel samples remain a rough legibility check, not proof of in-game
				readability. Slots, mass, capacity and final Light/Heavy sizes remain
				unresolved. The later direction below supersedes earlier storage-state
				and animation ideas.
			</p>

			<p>
				03 / External bus and 07 / Insulated layers are promising; 09 / Contact
				shoes also works in the small image. These are qualities to carry
				forward, not accepted whole designs. Orange is excluded from the storage
				palette: yellow, brand lime-yellow and an Android-like green remain
				candidates.
			</p>
			<h4>Direction clarified / engineered energy lattices</h4>
			<p>
				Storage means science-fiction energy held in lattices, surfaces or
				fields, rather than an electrical battery. Hexagonal lattice forms fit
				that idea, but their exact arrangement and construction remain open. The
				equipment is deliberately engineered: rugged ship hardware or exposed
				scientific apparatus, never improvised or haphazard.
			</p>
			<p>
				Exposed machinery must still look substantial enough to belong on a
				battleship and plausibly withstand a collision. This does not reverse
				the preference for visible construction without a protective enclosure.
				Structural members and working surfaces should convey strength
				themselves.
			</p>
			<p>
				The current storage direction has no visible charge-state indicators,
				blinking lights or animation. Remaining energy should not be disclosed
				through the exterior to an opponent. Earlier empty-state and
				animated-storage proposals are superseded; this does not settle
				animation for chargers or repair equipment. Yellow, brand lime-yellow
				and green remain material or identity candidates, not charge gauges.
			</p>
		</details>
		<details>
			<summary>02.6 / Unexecuted recovery proposal</summary>
			<p>
				The two attempts at 02.6 moved away from the useful references.
				Geometry, viewpoint, proportions and visual language changed together,
				making earlier judgments difficult to carry forward. Lattices and fields
				were interpreted as repeated hexagonal shapes before a convincing
				physical construction had been established. The resulting drawings did
				not answer the intended design question.
			</p>
			<p>
				The stable references are the exact 02.5 / 03 External bus and 07
				Insulated layers objects. Their exposed connections, insulation and
				separate working parts are promising; neither whole object is accepted.
				09 Contact shoes remains useful specifically for its small-scale
				appearance. The database-like impression of the repeated tilted slabs is
				still unresolved.
			</p>
			<p>
				The brief remains exposed, deliberately engineered, substantial
				science-fiction storage. Working surfaces hold energy; they are not
				ordinary electrical batteries. No charge display or storage animation is
				wanted. Yellow and green remain candidates, with orange excluded. These
				agreements do not prescribe hexagonal holes, a flat lattice icon or a
				new silhouette.
			</p>
			<h4>Next investigation / one construction change</h4>
			<p>
				Before another drawing, describe the apparent construction of 03 and 07
				and identify one physical relationship worth varying. A useful starting
				hypothesis is the relationship between each working surface, its edge
				thickness and its connection to the neighboring surface. Choose one of
				those relationships for a small comparison, retaining the reference's
				other geometry, viewpoint, scale and colors.
			</p>
			<p>
				The next example should show its unchanged reference beside a bounded
				variation, so the effect can be judged directly. Another nine-option
				grid is deferred until that comparison establishes a useful direction.
				This section records a discussion reset, not a delivered visual
				solution. Rendering correctness and build checks do not establish design
				fidelity.
			</p>
		</details>
	</section>
	<section>
		<h2>03–04 / Not reached</h2>
		<p>
			The study stopped without a readiness assessment or delivery scope. The
			recovery proposal above was not executed before closure.
		</p>
	</section>
</StudyLayout>

<style>
summary {
	cursor: pointer;
	font-weight: 600;
	padding-block: var(--spacing-md);
}
details {
	border-bottom: var(--flight-border);
	margin-block: var(--spacing-md);
}
</style>
